    <solution><m>\displaystyle \frac{10}{1-9 x} = 10 \cdot \sum_{n=0}^\infty (9 x)^n</m>.
Then we can
see that <m>c_n = 10 \cdot 9^n</m>, making
<m>c_0 = 10</m>, <m>c_1 = 10 \cdot 9 = 90</m>,
<m>c_2 = 10 \cdot 9^2 = 810</m>, etc.  

  



Regarding the radius of convergence, we perform the standard ratio test:
<me>\lim_{n\to\infty} \left| \frac{10(9 x)^{n+1}}{10(9 x)^n} \right|
=	\lim_{n\to\infty} \left| 9 x \right|
=	\left| 9 x \right|</me>
And then <m>\left| 9 x \right| \lt  1 \iff \left| x \right| \lt  \frac 1 {9}</m>,
making <m>R = \frac 1 {9}</m>.



</solution>
